[Music]  the white house says it will spend more  than  4 billion to fight covet 19 in native  american communities including  600 million for vaccinations this  morning first lady jill biden  is visiting the navajo nation the  largest native american reservation had  one of the country's highest per capita  death rates  last year more than 1200 of its people  have died of covet  now the territory is leading the nation  in vaccination rates and could be the  first in the u.s  to achieve herd immunity maria  villarreal  traveled to navajo nation in arizona and  new mexico  to check on the fight and the lessons  learned  the journey to reach herd immunity on  the navajo nation hasn't always been a  smooth one  we are going to a small community south  of shiprock new mexico  we're going to get smiles when we get  there nurse lyle lee drove nearly an  hour they give 72 year old jonah johnson  the first  dose there you go right there okay just  let it hang down  good job johnson was willing to take the  vaccine but refused to leave his rural  home  it's very important for us to do these  types of visits  you know and just respecting their  choice mckinley county new mexico  borders the navajo nation and is made up  of more than 70 percent native americans  in september of 2020 this county ranked  first in the state and sixth nationally  for cova deaths per capita at first  we didn't really have our guard up but  once it  hit within our family that's when  reality hit  reality struck quickly for jamie  barbone's family  her mother yvonne tolth got coven 19  first  one of my son he passed he spent like  a month and a half in the hospital  and he fought it but  didn't come home how hard was that for  you yvonne  i would get mad  and just cried a lot and my daughter  just  stood by me and my other kids  the high death toll led tribe officials  to spread the word about the importance  of vaccines  it spread like wildfire navajo nation  president jonathan nez was one of the  first to get his shot posting the  pictures  on social media he also hosts weekly  town halls to help educate nearly  300 000 members any outside entity i  think  of all people of color there's some  distrust with the federal government  and so i think when we started the  discussions  about getting the vaccines we were out  there daily almost  getting the information to our navajo  citizens but it wasn't just  providing that information the moment  they got access to vaccines  leaders split their efforts between mass  vaccination sites like this one  and meeting people out in their own  communities we had a rough time  in kovid but we fought very hard and we  have succeeded  to fight back kovid the tribe's chief  medical officer dr loretta christensen  says their efforts have led to more than  70 percent of navajo nation  receiving their first dose i think that  we are  either at herd immunity or very very  close are you smiling under your mouth i  am smiling yes  i am very proud of what we've done most  of jamie barbone's family has been  vaccinated  with the hope of preventing future  heartache why are you so set on taking  this  what my mom has went through and what  i've seen  i don't want to go through that it's all  about safety and family  that's why i feel 100 percent confident  and happy and safe that the vaccine is  here  even as the navajo nation reaches hurt  immunity they are not letting their  guard down  especially as states around them  continue to open up they are very  worried  about another potential surge so they  have put strict health guidelines in  place  that include a mask mandate and also an  overnight curfew  medeval cbs news window rock arizona  really good to hear because they were  hit so hard so early  and i guess they really you know decided  you know we've got to rely on ourselves  because they're  so isolated very impressive what they've  done and president nez who i've  interviewed a couple of times on cbsn  is exactly that impressive impressive

